===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ang|ƿæl}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} ''*walo-'' or ''*walu-'', from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} ''*welə-'' ‘wound, injure’. Cognate with Old Saxon ''wal-'' (in ''[[waldad|waldâd]]'' ‘murderous deed’), Old High German ''[[wal]]'', Old Norse ''[[valr]]''.

In Old Norse ''[[Valkyrie|valkyrja]]'', Old English ''[[wælcyrge]]''; literally "chooser of the slain".
The Germanic word may be cognate with Latin ''[[vulnus]]'' ‘wound’ and Old Irish ''[[fuil]]'' ‘blood’.

